<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna-indexrescale: a byte offset into an array becomes an element index
    (option indexrescale).

    Source (gcc -O2, x86-64):
      void fill(int *rule, int v, int two) {
        rule[0] = v;
        if (two) { rule[1] = v; rule[2] = v; }
        rule[two ? 3 : 1] = v;
        rule[4] = v;
      }

    gcc keeps the selected element as a byte offset, 4 or 0xc, and adds it to
    rule, which printed as *(uint4 *)((long)a0 + v1) = a1.

    Stage: P5, ActionIndexRescale: the offset's web is written only by
    constants that are multiples of 4 and read only as an offset from an int
    pointer, so the constants become 1 and 3 and the add a PTRADD.

    Pass 1 (default) prints a0[v1] = a1; pass 2 (indexrescale off) the byte
    offset.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000" readonly="true">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="fill"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401030" name="scan"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401060" name="_start"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu fill</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option indexrescale off</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="KUNA-INDEXRESCALE #1: the selected element is an index (default)" min="1" max="1">a0\[v1\] = a1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="KUNA-INDEXRESCALE #2: the index constants are element counts (default)" min="1" max="1">v1 = 3;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="KUNA-INDEXRESCALE #3: indexrescale off keeps the byte offset" min="1" max="1">a0 \+ v1\) = a1;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
